// =============================================================================
// stdlib.lumen — the standard-library pack (shared types + cross-cutting ops)
// =============================================================================
// Two parts: §1 the generalised annotation model (Annotation / Label / Comment),
// and §2 the standard OPERATIONS — search / query / batch / graph, et al. — that
// sit above the core primitive floor (get + list). §1:
//
// The generalised annotation model the issue pack's `computed labels` / `comments`
// projections resolve against. An Annotation is a rev-neutral resource that points
// AT a target resource and carries its own payload; Label and Comment are the two
// concrete annotations the issue pack leans on today, each generalised from a
// single target type to any resource.
//
// This refines the as-needed record landed with #403 (commit 07b65251): the §1
// OPEN is now RESOLVED (annotations are resources that SOFT-reference their target,
// not `rel<>` edges — see §1.0), and the rev-neutrality guarantee is stated
// normatively (§1, PRINCIPLE 1). One question remains, and it belongs to Core, not
// here: Core has no resource-level natural-key constraint yet (`unique` is rel-only),
// so Label's (target, name) key is spelled as intent and flagged CORE-OPEN (§1.2).
//
// SCOPE — what lives here:
//   Annotation — a rev-neutral resource that points AT a target resource and
//   carries its own payload. Label and Comment are the two concrete annotations
//   the issue pack leans on today.
//
// PRINCIPLES:
//   1. Annotations are rev-neutral w.r.t. their target. Creating, editing or
//      deleting an annotation NEVER advances its target's revision, emits no change
//      event on the target, and never enters the target's content hash. The
//      annotation carries its own identity, revision history and provenance; its
//      OWN revision advances normally. This is not a special rule — it falls out of
//      the target being untouched (an annotation is a separate resource; the target
//      does not name it), under Core's revision rule: "the revision changes exactly
//      when the resource's state changes" ([[core.lumen#Rev]]).
//   2. Point AT, don't own — and point SOFTLY. An annotation is a first-class
//      resource that names its target with a `target: ref<resource>` property. That
//      reference is SOFT, not structural: it tolerates a retired target and resolves
//      to its tombstone, and the target never cascades its retirement to the
//      annotation (contrast a `rel<>`'s structural `source`/`target`, which a scope
//      cascades or restricts — [[core.lumen#rel]]). The target's view of its
//      annotations (issue.lumen `labels` / `comments`) is a COMPUTED projection.
//   3. Projection is a reverse lookup, revision-transparent. A target's `labels` /
//      `comments` are computed by walking the `target` reference in REVERSE from the
//      target. Computing one never advances the target's revision, emits no event,
//      and never enters its content hash.
// =============================================================================

/// description: Free text — a nominal string.
type text is string


// #############################################################################
// §1. The model
// #############################################################################

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §1.0  RESOLVED — annotation is a resource that SOFT-references its target,
//       NOT a `rel<>` edge.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The #403 record left open whether an Annotation is better carried as a
// `rel<resource, …>` edge (like issue.lumen's Dependency) or as a resource with a
// `target` property. It is a resource with a `target` property. Three reasons, each
// grounded in Core:
//
//   1. A `rel<F, T>` is defined by its STRUCTURAL `source`/`target` pair — "nothing
//      distinguishes a relationship but its structural `source`/`target` pair"
//      ([[core.lumen#rel]]). An annotation has exactly ONE resource reference (its
//      target) plus value payload (`body`/`author`/`name`); there is no second
//      resource endpoint — a comment's `author` is a native string, not a ref. There
//      is no `source` resource to put on the left of a `rel<>`. Modelling it as a rel
//      would mean inventing a spurious endpoint.
//
//   2. Core already classifies this exact case. A reference that only points AT a
//      resource — "a note, an annotation" — is a SOFT reference, opposed to the
//      structural `source`/`target` of an edge ([[core.lumen#rel]], verbatim
//      example). A soft reference "tolerates a retired target and resolves to its
//      tombstone"; a structural endpoint never outlives its resource. An annotation
//      wants the soft semantics: a comment on a deleted issue should survive and
//      resolve to the issue's tombstone, not be cascade-retired and not pin the
//      issue open.
//
//   3. The payload IS the point. A rel's `{ edge-fields }` are properties OF the
//      relationship (Dependency's `createdBy`/`threadId`); the relationship without
//      its endpoints is nothing. An annotation's `body`/`name` are its reason to
//      exist; the target is the thing it is attached to. That is the shape of a
//      resource-with-a-soft-ref, not an edge.
//
// Discriminator for future packs: two resources genuinely related → `rel<>` (an
// edge, structural, keyed on the pair). One resource annotated by a free-standing
// payload → an Annotation (a resource, soft ref, keyed on target + its own fields).
//
// The issue pack KEEPS `labels` / `comments` as computed projections over this
// model (PRINCIPLE 3); it does not fold them into Issue.

/// description: Annotation — a rev-neutral resource that points AT a target resource and carries its own payload. `abstract` — only the concrete annotations below are instantiated.
/// normative: Rev-neutral w.r.t. the target. Writing, editing or removing an Annotation NEVER advances its `target`'s revision, emits NO change event on the target, and NEVER enters the target's content hash. This holds because the target is not touched by the write: the Annotation is a separate resource and the target does not name it. The Annotation has its own identity, revision history and provenance; its OWN revision advances normally, and its own write DOES emit a change event on the Annotation.
/// normative: `target` is a SOFT reference ([[core.lumen#rel]]): it is a plain `ref<resource>` body property, NOT the structural `source`/`target` of a `rel<>`. It tolerates a retired target and resolves to the target's tombstone; the target's retirement is never cascaded or restricted by an Annotation. An Annotation is NOT a field on the target — a target's view of its annotations is a COMPUTED projection (see issue.lumen `labels` / `comments`), computed by walking `target` in reverse.
abstract type Annotation is Bead {
  target: ref<resource>,          // the annotated resource — a SOFT reference
}

/// description: Comment — free-text commentary on a target resource.
/// normative: `author` is a native human/agent string — NOT a BDP attribution ref. `body` is the comment text. Two Comments on the same target are distinct resources (no natural key): a target may carry many, each with its own id and history.
type Comment is Annotation {
  body:    text,
  author?: text,                  // native author string — NOT a ref
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §1.2  Label — a named tag; the natural-key question is CORE-OPEN.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// description: Label — a named tag on a target resource. Its natural key is (`target`, `name`): at most one Label of a given `name` on a given target, and a same-key re-add is an upsert onto the one id.
/// normative: The natural key (`target`, `name`) is a RESOURCE natural-key constraint, NOT `unique` on a rel. `unique rel<F, T>` keys ONLY on the structural (`source`, `target`) pair and explicitly excludes body fields ([[core.lumen#rel]]); Label is not a rel, and `name` is a value, not a resource endpoint — so a rel's `unique` cannot express this key. The key must be carried by the resource, over one ref property (`target`) and one value property (`name`).
/// normative: CORE-OPEN — Core has no resource-level natural-key primitive today; `unique` is defined for `rel<>` only, where it derives the id deterministically from (`source`, `target`). Spelling Label's key therefore needs ONE of: (a) Core grows a resource natural-key modifier — the resource analogue of rel `unique`, deriving the id deterministically from a declared key tuple that may mix ref and value properties (e.g. `unique(target, name)`); or (b) the at-most-one-per-(target, name) invariant is enforced at the pack/operation layer (an upsert guard on label-add), NOT by a Core-derived id. (a) is preferred — it keeps the same-key-resolves-to-one-id replica guarantee that rel `unique` already gives Dependency. The decision is the Lumen scrub's; the intent is written here with the key tuple explicit so whichever spelling lands is a mechanical substitution.
type Label is Annotation {
  name: text,                     // natural key with `target` (see CORE-OPEN above)
}


// #############################################################################
// §2. The standard operations — search / query / batch / graph (et al.)
// #############################################################################
// The cross-cutting, non-domain capabilities that sit ABOVE the core primitive
// floor. Core's floor is `get` + `list(where: Filter)` — a flat conjunction, "the
// low bar every service supports" ([[core.lumen#list]]). These ops are the
// à-la-carte capabilities a service MAY advertise on top of it:
//
//   query  — the full boolean/traversal filter Core defers to "the stdlib": the
//            rich form of list's flat Filter (nested and/or/not + edge traversal).
//   search — ranked text search (lexical / semantic / hybrid) over bodies.
//   graph  — traverse rel<> edges from roots into a subgraph; the generic form of
//            issue.lumen's getDependencyTree, over ANY relationship.
//   batch  — bundle many invocations into one request (atomic or best-effort).
//   count  — the aggregate reduction (a scalar, no bodies) issue.lumen §2.A's
//            `count` verb lowers to.
//
// (`get-many` / `exists` from that §2.A note need no op of their own — a bulk read
// is a `batch` of `get`s or a `list` by id-set; existence is `count … > 0`.)
//
// Each is an INTRINSIC operation (native, no stored implementation), addressed by
// its well-known path under this pack (`stdlib/query`, …) and invoked through core
// `call` ([[core.lumen#call]]). A service lists the ops it offers in discovery;
// one it does not advertise is simply absent — a floor service offers none of
// these and `get` + `list` still work. The pack mounts its commands at the TOP of
// the aggregate `bd` tree (`cli.mount: "/"`), and each op carries a simple
// `cli.name`, so the catalog renders `bd query` / `bd search` / `bd graph` /
// `bd batch` / `bd count` directly ([[core.lumen#discovery]]). The service's bounds
// — supported `SearchMode`s, whether `via` traversal is offered, `maxGraphDepth`,
// `maxBatch` — ride in discovery as this pack's capability metadata.
//
// Error model (as §2.0 of issue.lumen): a safe query (`fn`) reads or faults with a
// `Problem` (malformed input, an unsupported capability) and declares no domain
// `E`; `batch` (`command`) carries each item's own `Outcome` and needs no
// top-level `E`. (`Scope` / `Filter` / `listCondition` / `selector` / `ReadRequest`
// / `Outcome` / `rep` / `source` / `PathString` / `FieldPath` are core types.)

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §2.1  query — list's rich sibling
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// description: The full query predicate — the rich form of list's flat [[core.lumen#Filter]]. A tree of boolean combinators over the same leaf conditions, plus edge traversal. `cond` reuses the base `listCondition` verbatim, so every `where: Filter` is a degenerate `all` of `cond`s — floor and capability speak ONE condition language.
type QueryExpr = union {
  /// description: Conjunction — every sub-expression must hold (an empty `of` is vacuously true).
  all  { of: [ QueryExpr ] }
  /// description: Disjunction — at least one sub-expression must hold.
  any  { of: [ QueryExpr ] }
  /// description: Negation.
  not  { expr: QueryExpr }
  /// description: A leaf — one base field condition ([[core.lumen#listCondition]]).
  cond { condition: listCondition }
  /// description: Traverse a relationship and match on the far end — holds when a neighbor reached over `edge` (a `rel<>` type-path) in `direction` satisfies `where`. The generic "has a neighbor such that" test.
  via  { edge: PathString, direction: "out" | "in" = "out", where?: QueryExpr }
}

/// description: One sort key over the result set; `sort` is an ordered list (the first key is primary).
type Sort = { field: FieldPath, order: "asc" | "desc" = "asc" }

/// description: The full query — list's rich sibling. The current resources in `scope` matching a boolean/traversal `QueryExpr`, ordered by `sort`, projected by `select`. The capability form of `list(where: Filter)`; a flat `Filter` is the degenerate case. Snapshot (`json`/`jsonl`; no `sse`). (`stdlib/query`; `cli.name: "query"` → `bd query`.)
/// normative: Read-or-fault — a malformed predicate faults `malformed-query`; no domain `E`. A service advertising `query` MUST accept every boolean combinator (`all`/`any`/`not`/`cond`); `via` traversal MAY be separately capability-gated and faults `query-capability-unsupported` when the service does not offer it.
fn query(scope: Scope, where: QueryExpr, sort?: [ Sort ], select?: selector, request?: ReadRequest) -> source<Resource>

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §2.2  search — ranked text search
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// description: How a search matches — `lexical` (substring / full-text, the floor for any `search` service), `semantic` (embedding similarity), or `hybrid` (both, fused).
type SearchMode = "lexical" | "semantic" | "hybrid"

/// description: One ranked search result — the matched resource, its relevance score (higher = better; the scale is service-defined and comparable only WITHIN one response), and an optional highlighted snippet.
type SearchHit = {
  resource: ref<resource>,
  score:    number,
  snippet?: string
}

/// description: Ranked text search over resource bodies in `scope` — the matches for `text`, best first. `where` pre-filters the corpus (the same predicate as `query`); `mode` picks lexical / semantic / hybrid; `select` expands each hit's resource inline. (`stdlib/search`; `cli.name: "search"` → `bd search`.)
/// normative: Read-or-fault — no domain `E`. `lexical` is the floor every `search` service offers; requesting a `mode` the service does not advertise faults `search-mode-unsupported`. Scores order ONE response and are not comparable across calls or services.
fn search(scope: Scope, text: string, where?: QueryExpr, mode?: SearchMode = "lexical", select?: selector, limit?: integer) -> [ SearchHit ]

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §2.3  graph — relationship traversal
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// description: One resource reached by a traversal, with the fewest hops from any root at which it was found.
type GraphNode = { resource: ref<resource>, depth: integer }

/// description: One relationship traversed — the edge resource itself and its endpoints, denormalized so a client renders the graph without re-reading each edge.
type GraphEdge = {
  edge:   ref<resource>,
  type:   PathString,
  source: ref<resource>,
  target: ref<resource>
}

/// description: The result of a `graph` traversal — the reachable nodes and the edges among them. A node appears ONCE (lowest depth); an edge that closes a cycle is still reported, so the shape is the actual graph, not a spanning tree.
type Subgraph = { nodes: [ GraphNode ], edges: [ GraphEdge ] }

/// description: Traverse relationships from one or more roots into a `Subgraph`. `edgeTypes` restricts which `rel<>` types to follow (type-path prefix; absent = all); `direction` follows `source→target` (`out`), `target→source` (`in`), or `both`; `depth` caps the hops. The generic form of issue.lumen's `getDependencyTree`, over any `rel<>`. (`stdlib/graph`; `cli.name: "graph"` → `bd graph`.)
/// normative: Read-or-fault — no domain `E`. Traversal is breadth-first and visits each node once, so a cyclic graph terminates and a cycle-closing edge is still emitted. `depth` is bounded by the advertised `maxGraphDepth`; a request above it faults `graph-depth-exceeded` rather than silently truncating.
fn graph(roots: [ ref<resource> ], edgeTypes?: [ PathString ], direction?: "out" | "in" | "both" = "out", depth?: integer = 1, select?: selector) -> Subgraph

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §2.4  batch — many invocations, one request
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// description: One invocation inside a `batch` — the op to run (a primitive name like `create`/`patch`/`delete`, or an operation path like `issue/claim`), its `target` resource where the op takes one, and its `arguments` (the same name/value shape as [[core.lumen#Request]]). `id` is a caller label echoed on the matching result.
type BatchItem = {
  id:        string,
  op:        PathString,
  target?:   ref<resource>,
  arguments: [ { name: string, value: any } ]
}

/// description: The fate of one `BatchItem`, correlated by `id` — the item's own `Outcome` (success / failure / fault / canceled).
type BatchResult = { id: string, outcome: Outcome<any, any> }

/// description: Run many invocations in one request. `atomic: false` (default) runs the items independently and best-effort — each result stands alone and partial success is normal. `atomic: true` runs them in one transaction — all commit or none. Items apply in array order. (`stdlib/batch`; `cli.name: "batch"` → `bd batch`.)
/// normative: The call returns `200` with one `BatchResult` per item, keyed by `id`; the HTTP status reports the batch TRANSPORT, never an item's fate (read each item's `outcome`). ATOMIC rollback: if any item settles as other than `success`, NOTHING commits, and every result reports either the triggering `failure`/`fault` or — for the siblings rolled back — `canceled { reason: "rolled-back" }`. So "did the batch commit?" is exactly "is every outcome a success?". A whole-request fault (malformed, or past `maxBatch`) faults with a `Problem` and returns no results. v0.2: items target ONE scope; cross-scope batching is out of scope.
command batch(items: [ BatchItem ], atomic?: bool = false) -> [ BatchResult ]

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §2.5  count — the aggregate reduction
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// description: The number of current resources in `scope` matching `where` — the aggregate reduction of `query`/`list`, computed service-side (no bodies returned). The op issue.lumen §2.A's `count` verb lowers to. (`stdlib/count`; `cli.name: "count"` → `bd count`.)
/// normative: Read-or-fault — no domain `E`. `where` is the full `QueryExpr`; a service offering only base `list` MAY accept a flat `Filter` (a degenerate `all` of `cond`s) and fault `query-capability-unsupported` on richer predicates.
fn count(scope: Scope, where?: QueryExpr) -> integer
